# The Caddyfile of development: one origin for the browser, on localhost.
# Caddy forwards /api to the API and everything else to Vite, both on the
# machine, and answers /up with the readiness of the API. The file storage
# has a host name of its own, files.localhost. Rules: Delivery, Health and
# Files in CODING_STANDARDS.md.
#
# The rules of delivery are snippets of delivery.caddyfile, beside this file,
# which the Caddyfile of staging imports too. Development imports every one
# but two: the built app, because Vite serves the app here, and the security
# headers, whose policy refuses the inline script and the style elements
# Vite writes into the page to reload it as the code changes.
#
# A site is a block of its own: an address and its directives. A host name
# for another service, such as the file storage, is a second block beside
# this one, `http://<name>.localhost { ... }`. Browsers send every name under
# localhost to the machine, so it needs no DNS record. Compose publishes one
# port for all of them.
#
# The upstreams can be named by the environment, as the tests of the API do.
# Compose leaves them at their defaults: the ports of the API and of Vite on
# the machine, which Docker reaches as host.docker.internal, and Garage, a
# container of the same network, by the name of its service.

import delivery.caddyfile

# The options of the whole Caddy: its logs carry no whole address of a
# caller. The comment above keeps `caddy fmt` from reading this block as one
# given to the import.
{
	import logs-without-whole-addresses
}

# Plain HTTP: development has no certificate, so no site redirects to HTTPS.
http://localhost {
	import site-of-the-app {$API_UPSTREAM:host.docker.internal:8080}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy {$WEB_UPSTREAM:host.docker.internal:5173}
	}
}

# The file storage, at a host name of its own: the browser sends and fetches
# the bytes of a file here, with a URL the API signed for this host name
# (Files in CODING_STANDARDS.md). The Host header reaches Garage as the
# browser sent it, because the signature covers it. Garage answers the
# cross-origin requests itself, from the rule of its bucket.
http://files.localhost {
	reverse_proxy {$FILES_UPSTREAM:garage:3900}
}
